insert into shop_notes (category, content, source_file) values
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Toolpath strategy notes: using a radius endmill, light stepover + heavy stepdown steps up once it hits the wall. For an insert/high-feed endmill, use light stepdown (~.03-.04) and heavier stepover (75-80% of cutter) instead, depending on cutter type. With a small carbide endmill with a corner radius, want to use the entire flute of the cutter. Interesting: finishing the floors in the roughing operation, then Waterline does the roughing surfaces -- this way you don''t need 2 different toolpaths to get steep and shallow machined.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Lesson 15 quick reference: Geo imports an mc file. Rough = OptiRough. Finish = Waterline, 2D contour, 3D pencil. Set Environment: setting stock is important for speeds/feeds -- set material to 6061 aluminum. Stock setup: bounding box, anchor to bottom, add .1 to top of stock. On the tool page, set feed calculation to "from material": From Tool uses speeds/feeds stored in the tool itself (values you enter); From Material has Mastercam calculate based on tool diameter + operation type + the surface footage/feedrate you told it for that material (works with stock defined).', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Material setup: Machine tab > Material tab > right-click ''Get from library'', grab your material, right-click it and Edit. Used 400 percent for carbide.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'OptiRough roughing toolpath: leave .010 stock on floor, select entire part, toolpath control from outside (works to inside). Use a bullnose (radius) 1 inch tool with .03125 radius. Right-click Edit tool, go to finalize properties, change material to carbide, click the calculator to update SFM/FPT (surfacing uses a percentage of total SFM, about x.8). Turn on RCTF. Adjust holder: B3E4-1000 (1 inch tool).', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'OptiRough cut parameters: 2.75 projection. Stepover .150, stepdown 7/8 (.875) -- heavy stepdown, light stepups. Important: step-up amount should be about the same as the tool''s corner radius (in this case .03). Big stepdowns, then once it steps over and hits a wall it starts stepping up. Steep/shallow: set max depth from the Z value of the floor. Linking parameters: clearance 3.0. Change rapids into high feedrate motions (G01 at 500 ipm) so it doesn''t dog-leg around the part and actually follows what you programmed (more on this in the dynamic milling course). Machine entire pass, not cutting any corners. Arc filtering: .005 total tolerance.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Arc filtering 50/50 setting: half is cut tolerance, half is filtering option (turns small line segments into arcs, fit to that tolerance). Check ''output 3D arc entry motion'' (leave disabled if your control doesn''t support helices).', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Waterline finishing toolpath: select entire part, leave 0 stock. Use a ball mill/sphere, 1/2 inch. Edit tool, change to carbide, press recalculate. Holder B3E4-0500 (1/2 inch, solid holder), tool projection 1.75. Cut parameters: stepdown .03 -- increase this if the computer is slow (fewer passes, faster processing) or decrease it for a better finish/smaller scallop height.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Waterline ''Add Cuts'': on flatter sections (Waterline is normally for more vertical faces) it adds cuts to maintain a specific stepover/scallop height, and cuts top/bottom of fillets more effectively (otherwise it can miss material if unchecked). Set .003 minimum stepdown, .03 maximum profile stepover. Enable Corner Rounding: rounds sharp corners instead of hitting them at 90 degrees, for a smoother motion at higher feedrates -- this leaves some material behind (hence the profile tolerance), but the pencil toolpath that comes after will clean it up. Steep/Shallow: Z coordinate of the bottom point. Linking parameters: 3.0 is good, full vertical retract, machine entire pass.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Waterline continued: full vertical retract, machine entire pass. Arc filter tolerance .001, 50/50, line/arc on in the XY plane. Note: lost some efficiency here because Waterline toolpaths are normally meant for vertical walls, so it slows/repeats more than it should on the radii -- necessary though, to avoid leaving excess material.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', '2D Contour finishing toolpath: created a point on the back of the part''s solid wall, 2D contour mode, click the loop around the part, make sure it''s climb milling. Use a 1/4 flat endmill, carbide, holder B3C4-0020 (collet-style), tool projection 2.5 (enough to reach the part).', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Tool projection setup: right-click the 1/4 tool and Edit Projection, click the point created, adjust the holder as close to the part body as possible (1.625), click enter to accept -- this updates the tool projection. Good way to verify/stay safe on how far the tool needs to stick out.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Pencil finishing toolpath (finish radii): right-click > select/add remaining entities, use boundary chain to make sure only the correct material is selected to be cut. Used pencil toolpath (3D), select the loop around the base of the material being machined. Used a 1/4 ball endmill, B3E4-0250 solid holder, 1.55 projection. Didn''t use stock -- used cut parameters + tool motion instead. Stepover is directly related to scallop height (changing one changes the other); set stepover .015.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Pencil toolpath rest-machining settings: reference tool diameter .53 (same idea as rest machining -- put in the size of the tool that roughed the part, or slightly bigger, which can create helpful overlap). Overthickness .03. Bitangency angle 165 -- tells Mastercam what counts as an intersecting face; if the tool doesn''t fit, this lets it treat it as a larger angle so it can still generate a toolpath (there''s a help button that explains this). Steep/shallow: lift the tool slightly off the face (.51) so it doesn''t cut a face it shouldn''t see. Linking parameters: force feedrate at 500 (check the feedrate box), keep tool down within 1.0 inch distance (so it feeds instead of retracting for short moves). Turn on arc filtering 50/50.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Verify: use Compare to see the difference between the solid file and the actual cut model.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Setup sheet: File > Configuration > Reports > Active Reports, make sure the setup sheet program dropdown is set to Active Reports, green check. In the toolpaths tab (left side), right-click > Setup Sheet -- it launches using whatever''s set in Configuration. Can leave everything at default and press okay.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Lesson 16 notes: will use material-based speed/feed calculation, define carbide tools, import a toolpath to save time and reuse a toolpath you like, use stock models to see where the tool is at (can define stock shape and start Verify from that toolpath) -- also useful for rest machining operations.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Lesson 16 setup: import the existing part file, do feed calculation from material. In the Machine tab, Material > right-click aluminum 6061 > Edit, make sure carbide is at 400%. New: in the Machine tab, select Configure Machine -- since the actual machine can''t reach the RPM Mastercam calculates, limit the max commandable RPM (expand Mill Spindle Group > keep expanding to the tool spindle > double-click it > default max is 50,000 RPM). Once set, loaded toolpaths adjust automatically.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Covering geometry so the toolpath can''t see it (e.g. for sinker EDM corners): Levels Manager > create covers > select covers/bounds in levels. Right-click, pick a wireframe color (orange). Wireframe tab > Curve One Edge -- places a wireframe line/spline/circle/arc on a surface edge; select the edges in complete shapes. Surfaces tab > create a flat boundary from that geometry, color it orange too. Simpler alternative (not as reliable): Surfaces tab > Fill Holes -- select the top and slide to the edge to cover.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Importing a roughing toolpath: right-click in blank toolpath-manager space > Import (set to import default toolpaths); to import from a specific Mastercam file, click the folder button. In the lower-right dropdown, select Educational or not depending on your Mastercam version (used Educational HLE here). Don''t need other boxes checked. Click the blue plus to apply, confirm it applied, then close.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Editing the imported toolpath: select everything (solid body + the covers made), select the perimeter of the part, strategy From Outside, compensate to center. Adjusted arc filtering tolerance to .005 (plus a few other small untracked tweaks).', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Create a stock model from a roughing toolpath, to drive rest machining: Toolpaths > Stock Model > give it a name > Stock Setup (auto-fills from stock dimensions) > Source Operations > select which toolpaths to apply to the stock > green check.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Rest roughing toolpath (OptiRough Dynamic): copy/reuse the roughing toolpath. Used a 1/4 bull with .03125 radius, carbide, recalculate. Holder B3E4-0250. Under Stock, select Rest Material (a separate operation) and pick the stock model you created. Note: there will be some tolerance noise/small steps left from the prior roughing operation (small scallops) -- you may not want a toolpath generated on every one of those scallops.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Remaining-stock adjustments for rest roughing: ignore small cusps, distance .012 (to start -- may need to experiment). Starting point for that number: the stock left on walls from the roughing op (e.g. .01 here), then increase by .001 at a time until it looks good.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Getting the tool into small covered crevices: go back to Model Geometry > Select Entities > deselect the cover you no longer want. In Toolpath Control, make sure Skip Pockets is off, or if on, the value is small enough. Cut Parameters: 150 percent stepdown and .012 stepup. Steep/shallow and linking parameters stay the same. Expand Linking Parameters > Entry Motion -- helix radius should already be autofilled.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Creating a stock model from simulation output: 2 ways. Better option: go into Simulator options and select the stock model from there. Worse option: Machine Group > Simulation tab > Stock Override selection > press the red ''step material'' button, select the roughing op, OK/OK, launch the 2nd toolpath. Best practice: just select the stock + toolpath directly in the Toolpaths tab. Create another stock model from the finished roughing path, select a different display color for it so you can see what each toolpath actually did (only the selected operation''s portion shows in the new color).', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', '2D Area Mill (high-speed 2D) for finishing flat surfaces, used for advanced chaining options. If machining regions are left blank, it defaults to using the stock setup as the machining region. Strategy From Outside, select avoidance regions (loop around the bottom of the part) to exclude. Use a 1/2 flat endmill for the inside toolpath -- important: right-click in the blank tool-list space and Reinitialize first. Holder B3E4-0500, projection 1.75. Cut parameters: 0 stock on floors, .01 on walls, stepover 65 percent (a range, not exact), keep tool down within 1.0 distance. Linking parameters had some odd incremental patterns.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Analyze Dynamic (Home tab, arrow + question mark icon) to identify part dimensions: turn on face selection, click a face/corner of a pocket to see its radius/diameter values (e.g. .1 radius means use a .1875 tool). Use this to find one radius tool that can work for the whole part. Preferred a radius endmill over a small ball endmill (bigger, more rigid, doesn''t need to stick out as far). Used 3/16 flat for the profile; 1/32 radius bull for the radii (though a bigger endmill may work too).', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Finishing open pockets (2 places): normal pocket milling operation, make sure Partial Loop is selected (select the beginning and end of the loop, or select outer shared edges -- outer shared edges is the better option) and click the face of the pocket to mill. Used a 3/16 flat endmill, holder B3E4-0187, projection 1.5. Cut parameters: 0 stock on walls/floors, overlap 125 percent, pocket type = Open, with ''use open pocket cutting method'' and ''use standard pocket for closed chains'' checked. Roughing: 65 percent stepover.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Finishing open pockets continued: uncheck ''machine finish passes after roughing''. Turn off leads (lead in/lead out). For ''select for top of stock'', any point at the top of the pocket being machined works.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Finishing open radius pockets: used op6 as source, for the 2 smaller pockets, partial loop selected. Cut parameters: had to ''lie'' to the operation to get the cut right -- cut an extra .031 deeper than already chained, with -.031 thousandths stock to leave on the wall (places the OD on the wall). Everything else the same as before.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Contour finishing of the mold base: climb mill, 3/16 flat endmill. Create a new stock model from the 2D toolpath (labeled ''2D Finishing Done''), stock-model radio button, source operations = ops 5, 6, 7, and 8.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Semi-finish using Surface High Speed Equal Scallop (3D equal scallop) -- a catch-all toolpath that looks at both steep and shallow areas. For the finishing toolpath that leaves no stock behind: select avoidance geometry (selected all the stock the jaws are holding onto).', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Equal Scallop cut parameters: 3/8 ball endmill. Checked: optimize cut order, reverse cutting order, smoothing. Stepover .025 was good enough.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Finish a circular feature using Surface High Speed Spiral: go back to level 1, turn off visibility of level 2, use the Spiral toolpath. 0 stock. (Check the program to see what changed.) Scallop .0005. Select the center point, define to the outside radius. Linking parameters: minimum vertical retract.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Stock model for the pencil toolpath: stock models built with tolerance can stack up tolerance/inconsistency over time if reused repeatedly -- create a fresh stock model instead, applying every toolpath created so far in Source Operations.', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf'),
  ('MasterCam / Gibbs Software Notes', 'Finish using Surface High Speed Pencil (rest passes): pencil operation sees wall intersections and runs toolpaths along the fillets of those walls. Used a 3/16 ball endmill. Under Stock: trim to stock on, select one other operation and use the stock just made, ignore small cusps on, distance .002. Cut parameters: .0005 scallop, reference tool diameter .375 (from that tool), thickness .02, bitangency angle 175 (the relationship between wall and floor, and where the toolpath can occur).', 'Lesson_15_and_16_notes.pdf');